Here is an example of a React component that provides autocomplete functionality without relying on other third-party packages:
```
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

const Autocomplete = ({ suggestions, onSelect }) => {
  const [inputValue, setInputValue] = useState('');
  const [suggestionsList, setSuggestionsList] = useState([]);

  useEffect(() => {
    const timer = setTimeout(() => {
      const filteredSuggestions = suggestions.filter((suggestion) => {
        return suggestion.toLowerCase().includes(inputValue.toLowerCase());
      });
      setSuggestionsList(filteredSuggestions);
    }, 300);

    return () => {
      clearInterval(timer);
    };
  }, [inputValue, suggestions]);

  const handleInputChange = (event) => {
    setInputValue(event.target.value);
  };

  const handleSelect = (suggestion) => {
    onSelect(suggestion);
    setInputValue('');
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <input
        type="text"
        value={inputValue}
        onChange={handleInputChange}
        placeholder="Type to search..."
      />
      <ul>
        {suggestionsList.map((suggestion, index) => (
          <li key={index} onClick={() => handleSelect(suggestion)}>
            {suggestion}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
};

export default Autocomplete;
```
Here's an explanation of how the component works:

* The component takes two props: `suggestions` (an array of strings to suggest) and `onSelect` (a function to call when an item is selected).
* The component uses two state variables: `inputValue` (the current input value) and `suggestionsList` (the filtered list of suggestions).
* When the input value changes, the component uses `setTimeout` to wait 300ms before filtering the suggestions array and storing the result in `suggestionsList`.
* The component uses `useEffect` to set up the debounced filtering and clear any existing timeout when the component is re-rendered.
* The component renders an `input` element with a `value` prop set to `inputValue` and an `onChange` prop set to `handleInputChange`.
* The component renders an `ul` element containing `li` elements for each suggestion in `suggestionsList`. Each `li` element has an `onClick` prop set to `handleSelect`, which calls the `onSelect` function with the selected suggestion and updates the `inputValue` state.

